-- title:
--   Nonzero primes of a Weierstrass coordinate ring are points
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be an algebraically closed field and let $W$ be a Weierstrass curve over $K$ in affine form, with coordinate ring `W.CoordinateRing`, i.e. $K[X][Y]$ modulo the Weierstrass polynomial $W(X,Y)$. Let $P$ be an ideal of this coordinate ring which is prime and different from $\bot$. The assertion is that there exist $a, b \in K$ such that `W.Equation a b` holds, i.e. $(a,b)$ satisfies the affine Weierstrass equation of $W$, and such that $P$ is the ideal `XYIdeal W a (C b)`, the ideal generated by the classes of $X - a$ and of $Y - b$ in the coordinate ring. No smoothness or nonsingularity assumption on $W$ is imposed, and the prime $P$ is not assumed maximal; maximality is part of what the proof establishes along the way.
--
--   This is Hilbert's Nullstellensatz in the form describing the nonzero points of the spectrum of the affine coordinate ring of a plane Weierstrass cubic over an algebraically closed field: they are exactly the vanishing ideals of the affine $K$-points of the curve. It is the basic dictionary underlying divisors, orders of vanishing and function-field constructions on elliptic curves, and is invoked in the treatment of isogenies, their dual data and norms, and of period pairs.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_WeierstrassCurve_Affine_CoordinateRing_exists_eq_XYIdeal.lean

import Mathlib
